# UserSetupEnvironment -- Haiku on AWS Graviton.
#
# Sourced by /system/boot/SetupEnvironment (see its lines 48-49), which
# data/launch/user applies with `env` to the whole `desktop` target. So this is
# the one place that puts a variable into the environment of *every* service in
# the user session, which is exactly what is needed here.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# A Nitro instance has no framebuffer, so the only Desktop app_server can build
# is a remote one, and a Desktop is keyed on (uid, TARGET_SCREEN). An application
# sends its own getenv("TARGET_SCREEN") when it asks for a Desktop
# (src/kits/app/AppMisc.cpp:223), and AppServer::_FindDesktop() only returns an
# existing Desktop when that string matches.
#
# data/launch/user launches Tracker and Deskbar as *services*, and
# SetupEnvironment does not set TARGET_SCREEN. So without this file they ask for
# a Desktop with target == NULL, get no match, and
# AppServer::_CreateDesktop(uid, NULL) fails inside Desktop::_Init():
# ScreenManager::AcquireScreens() finds no unowned screen (there is no
# framebuffer) and cannot create one either, because that branch requires a
# non-NULL target. It returns B_ENTRY_NOT_FOUND, _Init() returns early, and the
# Desktop never finishes construction:
#
#     app_server: Failed to initialize virtual screen configuration:
#     No such file or directory
#
# The app then exits, launch_daemon restarts the service with no back-off, and
# the result is a hot loop -- roughly 185 teams per second, and about 145 copies
# of that message per console interval, which is what dominated every serial log
# of the watchdog campaign. Nothing ever draws, because the object that owns the
# window list never exists.
#
# Setting it here fixes the cause rather than the symptom: launch_daemon's own
# Tracker and Deskbar join the remote Desktop, so no second launcher is needed
# and remote-desktop.sh no longer has to start them.
#
# The value is a bare port number, matching what src/apps/remotedesktop
# exports (RemoteDesktop.cpp:139). Keep it in step with
# HAIKU_REMOTE_DESKTOP_PORT in remote-desktop.sh and with DESKTOP_PORT in
# desktop-client/connect-desktop.sh.
export TARGET_SCREEN=10900
